<sn><md>### NIC 

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Network Interface Card
A **NIC** is a hardware component that allows a computer or other device to connect to a network.

**Functions:**

* Connects a device to a network.
* Converts computer `data into signals` that can travel through the network medium.
* Receives network signals and converts them back into data.
  ```
  data ⇄ signal
  ```
* Provides a `MAC address` for network communication.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0736/7566/9719/files/SEOon_NICs_Basic_Knowledge.jpg?v=1758523240)
:::
:::

**Types:**
* Ethernet NIC — wired connection
* Wireless NIC — Wi-Fi connection

**Example:** The Ethernet port or Wi-Fi adapter in a laptop is associated with its NIC.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Hub

A **hub** is a basic networking device used to `connect multiple devices in a local network`.

It has multiple ports by which its bind multiple computer together and form local area network (LAN) . When any new data appar in the hub it copies that data to all port and send to all devices connected with it . When a hub receives data, it `broadcasts the data to every connected device`.The devices check the destination information and determine whether the data is intended for them.


:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-7
**Characteristics:**

* Works mainly at `OSI Layer 1` — Physical Layer.
* Does not intelligently `!determine the destination` device.
* Creates more `unnecessary network traffic`.
* Generally operates using a `shared communication medium`.
* Mostly `obsolete` in modern networks.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-5
![img](https://images.ctfassets.net/aoyx73g9h2pg/2BeY1nsgq7F1zV7TZezCeV/a01a704bbe01389eebedccdfe285801b/What-is-a-Computer-Network-Hub-Diagram.jpg)
:::
:::

+++ ...
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/hub1.png)
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/hub2.png)
+++</md></sn><sn><md>### Switch

A **switch** `connects multiple devices` within a LAN and forwards data to the [specific destination device]{.fg-teal}.

Swich also have multiple port by which it bind multiple computer and create local area network (LAN).Whenever a new data received in the switch it does not copied or send that data to all devices instead it has a mac address table of its connected devices.

Unlike a hub, a switch learns the **MAC addresses** of connected devices and uses its MAC address table to decide where to forward frames.

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/switch2.png)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**Characteristics:**

* Mainly operates at `OSI Layer 2` — Data Link Layer.
* Uses **MAC addresses**.
* `Reduces unnecessary traffic` compared with a hub.
* Each switch port can provide a `separate collision domain`.
* Modern Ethernet switches are commonly used in LANs.
:::
:::

+++ ...
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/switch1.png)
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/switch2.png)
+++
</md></sn><sn><md>### Switch vs Hub

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/hub2.png)
:::
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/switch2.png)
:::
:::

Both switch and hub has ports by which they connect local computer and share information (LAN).Their main difference is that hub sends received data to host and switch only send data to target destination.

| Feature               | Hub                                             | Switch                                             |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| **Basic function**    | Connects multiple devices in a network          | Connects multiple devices in a network             |
| **OSI Layer**         | Layer 1 — Physical                              | Layer 2 — Data Link                                |
| **Address used**      | Does not use MAC addresses for forwarding       | Uses MAC addresses                                 |
| **Data unit**         | Bits/signals                                    | Ethernet frames                                    |
| **Data forwarding**   | Sends data to all connected ports               | Sends data to the intended destination port        |
| **Intelligence**      | Less intelligent                                | More intelligent                                   |
| **Traffic**           | Creates more unnecessary traffic                | Reduces unnecessary traffic                        |
| **Collision domain**  | All ports share one collision domain            | Each port is generally a separate collision domain |
| **Bandwidth**         | Shared among connected devices                  | Dedicated per port in typical full-duplex Ethernet |
| **Performance**       | Lower                                           | Higher                                             |
| **Security**          | Less secure because traffic is broadly repeated | Better isolation of unicast traffic                |
| **MAC address table** | No                                              | Yes                                                |
| **Duplex**            | Typically half-duplex                           | Typically full-duplex                              |
| **Use today**         | Mostly obsolete                                 | Commonly used in modern LANs                       |
| **Example**           | Basic Ethernet hub                              | 8-port/24-port Ethernet switch                     |

+++ ...
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/hub-switch-router1.png)
**collision domain** : A collision domain is a part of a network where two or more devices can transmit data at the same time and their signals may collide.
+++
</md></sn><sn><md>### Router

A **router** `connects different networks` and determines where packets should be sent.

A router examines the **destination IP address** of a packet and uses its routing information to determine the next destination.

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
**Functions:**

* Connects different networks.
* Uses **IP addresses**.
* Determines `suitable paths` for packets.
* Separates broadcast domains.
* Can perform `NAT, DHCP, firewalling`, and other functions depending on the device.
* Most router have built in switch inside them.
* There are combined hardware that serve both router,modem and switch functionality. 

**Example:** Your home Wi-Fi router connects your home LAN to your ISP's network and ultimately the Internet.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/router8.png)
:::
:::


